;;; A positive derived under `(absent (q one))` survives into a world that
;;; commits `(q one)` — M1e2 **W8**, contested. Register row
;;; `W8/stale-naf-fork` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(p one)` is derived at root because `(q one)` is absent. `p` and `q`
;;; together are a contradiction. The goal is `q`, so the rung proposes
;;; `(q one)`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine says ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   Solution k = 1, `{(p one)}` plus `(not (q one))`, `exhausted = true`
;;;
;;; Committing `(q one)` falsifies `derive-p`'s guard, but nothing retracts
;;; (`absent_semantics.md`: *retraction / truth maintenance: not provided*), so
;;; the fork holds the stale `(p one)` beside `(q one)`, the clash fires, and
;;; the lookahead kills the candidate. The state `{(q one)}` is unreachable.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand: a reading ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Q-M1e2.2 — does a guard's `absent` premise bind the state the search ends
;;; in?
;;;
;;;   guards are premises   committing `(q one)` withdraws `(p one)`, the
;;;                         clash never fires, `{(q one)}` is alive, and root
;;;                         is not maximal: **`Solution`, k = 1, `{(q one)}`**.
;;;   append-only           today's answer is right: the fork with both is
;;;                         dead, and root is the solution.
;;;
;;; The program is stratified (`q` is no rule's conclusion), so the third
;;; reading — refuse what cannot be stratified — does not reach it.
;;;
;;; Banked from M1e2's probes at S1e2.1.1.
(relation thing T)
(relation p T)
(relation q T)
(thing one)
(rule derive-p ()
  :match  (and (thing ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (p ?x)
  :priority 200)
(rule p-and-q-clash ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (q ?x))
  :assert (false)
  :priority 300)
(query :goal (q ?x))
